

:: day_5_14_str

The white swallowed everything   vision, sound, gravity, the feeling of Avan's fingers around mine   and for a span of time I couldn't measure, I existed nowhere. No body. No floor. No breath. Just the searing totality of light pressing against my closed eyelids like the sun had crawled inside the room and detonated.

Then it dimmed.

Not suddenly. Gradually   the way a surgical lamp powers down, halo contracting, edges sharpening   and the first thing I saw was chrome. My bare feet on chrome. Polished black, threaded with pulse-veins that glowed a pale, sterile blue-white, nothing like the warm violets from before. Clinical. Cold. The light of an operating theater.

I was standing on a circle. A raised platform no wider than a dinner table, embedded in the apartment floor, its rim outlined in hard white light. My toes curled against the cold surface. My legs were trembling   thighs pressed together, that unfamiliar softness compressing, hips canted at an angle my spine still didn't know how to hold.

Six feet to my left   another circle. Avan.

He stood rigid, shoulders hunched forward as though trying to fold that enormous frame into something smaller. His fists hung at his sides, knuckles bone-white. The sterile light carved every new ridge of him into sharp relief   the stacked slabs of his chest, the corded forearms, the jaw that could have been chiseled from marble. His eyes   Ava's eyes, forest-green and drowning   stared straight ahead at nothing.

<<leona "Ava…?">>

My voice came out thin. Cracked. A sound I still couldn't reconcile with anything that should come from me.

His head turned. Slow. Mechanical. Like his neck had to remember how to rotate under all that new mass.

<<avan "I'm here.">>

Two words. Bass so deep I felt it vibrate in my sternum. His gaze dropped to me   all of me   and I watched his pupils blow wide before he jerked his eyes to the floor. The pulse-veins beneath his platform flickered   a sharp spike of cyan. The apartment reading him. Broadcasting.

<<system "Familiarization Guide   Module 1: System Introduction and Body Overview. Initiating.">>

The voice came from everywhere. Walls. Floor. Ceiling. The chrome itself seemed to speak   flat, genderless, stripped of every trace of warmth or personality the welcome system had carried when they'd first shown us Eidolon City in the store. That version had been inviting. Encouraging. This version sounded like a machine reading an autopsy report.

Around us, the holographic mirrors activated.

Not two. Not four. A full three-hundred-sixty-degree arc   panels of light clicking on in sequence, chunk chunk chunk chunk, each one angling inward until we stood at the center of a circular cage of reflections. Every mirror showed a different angle. Every angle showed too much.

I saw myself from the front   honey-brown waves spilling past my waist, breasts straining against the thin synth-fabric the system had wrapped me in, hips wider than the platform circle, legs pressed together in a pose that screamed please stop looking at me. I saw myself from the side   the dramatic curve of my chest jutting forward, the impossible dip of my waist, the swell of my backside rounding out behind me. From the back   the long sweep of hair, the narrow shoulders, the hourglass silhouette that tapered and flared like a figure drawn by someone who'd never heard the word restraint.

Every mirror. Every angle. Inescapable.

<<leona "Turn them off   turn them OFF ">>

<<system "Mirrors are required for visual-somatic synchronization. They will remain active throughout the guide.">>

Avan's mirrors showed him the same mercy. None. His front reflection was a wall of muscle   broad chest, ridged abdomen, arms thick enough to bend steel. His side profile was a landscape of hard angles   the shelf of his brow, the column of his throat, the dramatic V-taper from shoulders to waist. I saw his eyes darting between reflections, searching each one for something familiar, something Ava   and finding only Avan staring back with a face that belonged on the cover of a fantasy novel.

<<avan "This isn't   I don't   that's not ME ">>

<<system "Correct. That is your current avatar, designated AVAN MORGAN. Your original profile, AVA MORGAN, was lost during a data corruption event at session initialization.">>

A beat of silence. Then two holographic silhouettes materialized between us   projected from the floor, rising like ghosts made of light. One feminine. One masculine. Life-sized, translucent, rotating slowly.

The feminine silhouette was mine. Leona's outline drawn in cool blue light   every curve, every proportion mapped to the millimeter. Height markers appeared beside it: 5'6". Weight: 132 lbs. Body type classification scrolling in small clinical text: hourglass, high curvature index, elevated soft-tissue ratio.

The masculine silhouette was Avan's. Broader, taller, dense with projected muscle fiber. Height: 6'3". Weight: 188 lbs. Body type: mesomorphic, high muscle-density index, athletic-dominant structure.

<<system "During the initialization failure, the system was unable to recover your stored avatar profiles. Emergency protocol accessed subconscious user data to generate replacement templates.">>

<<leona "We didn't CONSENT to that ">>

<<system "Acknowledged. Consent was not obtained. The system's emergency protocol prioritized session stability over user authorization. This is logged as an incident. However, avatar reversal is not possible until the Eidolon City primary questline is completed.">>

Avan made a sound   low, guttural, the kind of noise a caged animal makes when it realizes the door isn't opening. His hands came up and pressed flat against his face, fingers dragging down the unfamiliar terrain of sharp cheekbones and heavy jaw.

<<avan "So we're just   stuck? In THESE?">>

<<system "The guide exists to prevent exactly the distress you are currently experiencing. Abrupt avatar divergence of this magnitude carries significant risk of psychological desynchronization   a condition in which the user's cognitive self-model rejects the physical avatar.">>

The silhouettes between us pulsed   drawing attention back to themselves. New data began populating around each figure, scrolling in columns of cold white text.

<<system "Psychological desync manifests in three escalating stages. Stage one: persistent disorientation, impaired motor function, sensory mismatch triggering nausea and vertigo. Stage two: acute dissociative episodes, loss of proprioceptive awareness, inability to distinguish avatar sensation from phantom sensation. Stage three: full somatic rejection   the user's mind ceases to recognize the avatar as self, resulting in catatonic shutdown.">>

My blood ran cold. Colder than the chrome under my feet. Colder than the sterile light washing every surface.

<<leona "Catatonic   you mean we could just… shut down? Go brain-dead in here?">>

<<system "VR-induced catatonia is reversible upon session termination. However, session termination requires either voluntary logout   currently locked   or external intervention from a non-immersed individual.">>

Avan's voice dropped to something barely above a whisper. Controlled. Tight. The voice of someone holding a scream between their teeth.

<<avan "No one knows we're in here. Jamie and Rowan think we haven't started yet.">>

The weight of that settled over us like a physical thing. The mirrors reflected two people who understood, simultaneously, that they were alone in a locked system inside bodies that could break their minds if they didn't learn to live in them. Fast.

<<system "The Familiarization Guide is designed to prevent desync by systematically introducing you to your avatar's physical parameters. Completion of all modules is mandatory before logout access is restored.">>

<<leona "How many modules?">>

<<system "Fifteen.">>

<<avan "Fif  FIFTEEN?!">>

<<system "Your avatars diverge from your original bodies in every major physical category. The guide must be thorough to ensure your safety. An incomplete understanding of your avatar increases desync probability by 340%.">>

The holographic silhouettes rotated again   and this time, the data changed. The cool blue outlines began highlighting specific regions, color-coded, each one tagged with clinical annotations.

Leona's silhouette lit up in sections,

Skeletal structure   highlighted in pale green. Text scrolled: Pelvic width increased 38% from original user. Shoulder width decreased 14%. Spinal curvature adjusted for anterior pelvic tilt. Rib cage narrowed. Femoral angle widened.

<<system "Your skeletal structure has been completely reconstructed. User Leo Hart's original male pelvis   narrow, vertically oriented   has been replaced with a female pelvis: wider, shallower, tilted forward. This single change alters your center of gravity, your gait, your balance, your seated posture, and your ability to bear weight.">>

[[Continue Module 1|day_5_14_str_1]]

:: day_5_14_str_1

I stared at the green-lit bones rotating inside my silhouette. My bones. Except they weren't. They were architecture I'd never lived in, arranged around organs I'd never carried, supporting weight I'd never felt.

Muscle distribution   highlighted in warm amber. Text: Total muscle mass decreased 41%. Distribution shifted from upper-body dominant to lower-body dominant. Grip strength reduced approximately 50%. Quadricep and gluteal mass increased relative to total composition.

<<system "You have significantly less upper body strength than your original avatar. Movements that previously required minimal effort   lifting, pulling, pushing   now demand proportionally greater exertion. Your lower body, however, has increased in relative power. Your thighs and glutes are your strongest muscle groups.">>

Fat distribution   highlighted in soft rose. Text: Subcutaneous fat increased 62%. Primary deposits: breasts, hips, thighs, buttocks, lower abdomen. Visceral fat decreased.

<<system "Your body now stores fat subcutaneously   beneath the skin   rather than viscerally around internal organs. This creates the soft tissue layer you are currently experiencing across your chest, hips, thighs, and buttocks. This tissue is not removable. It is fundamental to your avatar's structural design.">>

I hugged myself tighter. Felt the give of it   the softness that compressed under my arms, the breasts that shifted and pressed together. Every word the system spoke made the body more real and less mine simultaneously.

Nerve density   highlighted in electric violet. Text: Cutaneous nerve density increased 220% across all epidermal surfaces. Concentration zones: lips, nipples, inner thighs, vulvar region, fingertips.

<<system "This is critical. Your avatar's skin contains more than double the nerve endings of your original body. You will experience touch, temperature, texture, pressure, and pain at significantly heightened levels. Certain areas   marked in the display   carry extreme sensitivity. Accidental stimulation of these zones may produce involuntary physical responses including vocalization, muscle contraction, and visible arousal indicators.">>

My cheeks burned. The pulse-veins in the nearest wall caught it   flaring rose-pink, broadcasting my humiliation across every reflective surface in the room. I bit my lower lip and the sensation that raced through the nerve-dense tissue was so sharp I gasped.

Avan's silhouette was running a parallel analysis. His jaw tightened with every new highlight.

Skeletal structure   green: Height increased 28 cm. Shoulder width increased 37%. Pelvic width decreased 12%. Bone density increased across all major groups.

<<system "User Ava Morgan's original female skeleton has been replaced with a male structure significantly larger and denser than average. Your shoulders are now your widest point. Your hips have narrowed. Your center of gravity has shifted upward   from the pelvis to the chest. This will affect every aspect of movement, balance, and spatial awareness.">>

Muscle distribution   amber: Total muscle mass increased 89%. Upper body dominant. Grip strength approximately 2.4x original user.

<<system "You are dramatically stronger than your original body. Your grip alone could fracture bone with uncontrolled force. Every physical interaction   touching objects, touching people   must be recalibrated for this level of strength. Failure to adjust risks unintentional harm to yourself and others.">>

Avan looked at his hands. Turned them over. Massive palms, long fingers, tendons standing taut under bronze skin. He curled them into fists and the mirror showed forearms that flexed with terrifying definition.

<<avan "I could hurt someone without even meaning to…">>

<<system "That is precisely why this guide exists.">>

Fat distribution   rose: Subcutaneous fat decreased 54%. Minimal deposits. Visceral fat marginally increased. Body fat percentage: approximately 12%.

<<system "Your avatar carries very little soft tissue. The leanness exposes muscle definition, vascular structure, and bone architecture. You will notice reduced cushioning when sitting, lying down, or absorbing impact. Hard surfaces will feel harder. Cold will penetrate faster.">>

Nerve density   violet: Cutaneous nerve density decreased 38% across general epidermis. Concentration zones: glans penis, frenulum, perineum, inner thighs.

<<system "Your overall skin sensitivity has decreased compared to your original body. However, specific genital zones carry extreme concentrated sensitivity. Incidental contact with these areas   through clothing friction, movement, or accidental touch   may produce involuntary physical responses including erection.">>

Avan's face darkened   not with embarrassment but with a deep, startled dread. His reflection in the mirrors showed it clearly: a powerful man flushing beneath bronze skin, eyes too wide, hands opening and closing at his sides with the barely restrained panic of someone realizing how much they don't know about the machine they're piloting.

<<system "Organ configuration.">>

Both silhouettes pulsed   interiors illuminating now. Cross-sections. The cold blue light peeled our holographic doubles open like anatomical textbooks.

<<system "User Leona Hart: internal reproductive organs are fully modeled. Uterus, ovaries, fallopian tubes, and vaginal canal are present and functional. Hormonal cycling is active. This avatar is capable of ovulation, menstruation, conception, and pregnancy.">>

<<leona "P-pregnancy?! I'm   I was a GUY twenty minutes ago!">>

<<system "Your original sex is irrelevant to your current avatar's biological capability. This body is female. It functions as female. The guide will address reproductive considerations in a later module.">>

<<system "User Avan Morgan: internal and external reproductive organs are fully modeled. Testes, vas deferens, prostate, seminal vesicles, and penile tissue are present and functional. Hormonal production is active. This avatar is capable of erection, ejaculation, and fertilization.">>

Avan made a choked sound. Not a word. Just   air leaving his lungs like he'd been punched. His huge hand came up and covered his face, fingers trembling against his forehead. In the mirror behind him, the neon veins spiked white   reading a heart rate that had to be redlining.

<<avan "I can… I could get someone…">>

He couldn't finish the sentence.

<<system "The guide will address all reproductive and sexual parameters in dedicated modules. For now, understand that your avatars are not costumes. They are fully realized biological systems. They breathe. They metabolize. They respond to stimuli. They carry every capacity and every vulnerability of the sex they represent.">>

Silence.

The mirrors kept rotating through angles. The silhouettes kept pulsing with data. The apartment's clinical light showed us everything   every inch of what we'd become, annotated, measured, catalogued in floating text that didn't care how we felt about it.

<<system "This concludes Module 1: System Introduction and Body Overview. Wrist restraints will now deactivate. Ankle restraints will reduce to passive tracking. The apartment door and logout function remain locked until all fifteen modules are completed.">>

The violet rings around my wrists dissolved   just evaporated, their light scattering into tiny motes that drifted down and were absorbed by the floor's pulse-veins. My arms fell to my sides. Free. The relief was so sharp it hurt. I rubbed my wrists   delicate wrists, thin enough that my new fingers wrapped all the way around them   and the friction of skin on skin sent a cascade of sensation racing up my forearms because apparently even that was amplified now.

Avan's wrist restraints dissolved simultaneously. He rolled his massive shoulders, the joint cracking with an audible pop that echoed off the chrome walls. His ankle bands dimmed from hard violet to a faint pulsing glow   still there, still tracking, but no longer pinning him in place.

<<system "You may move freely within the apartment. Module 2 will begin in three minutes. Hydration and climate controls are available. Use this interval to acclimate to basic standing posture in your current form.">>

The clinical lighting eased   not all the way back to the warm neon of before, but enough to stop the room from feeling like a surgical theater. Violet crept back into the wall-veins, tentative, like blood returning to frozen fingers. The city outside the windows resumed its slow, hypnotic pulse. Neon rain. Holographic towers. A world that didn't know or care that two people had just been stripped to their blueprints and told they were trapped inside bodies their own minds might reject.

Avan crossed the distance between our platforms in two long strides   covering ground that would have taken Ava six steps   and stopped in front of me. He towered. The top of my head barely reached his collarbone. His shadow fell over me and I tilted my chin up   way up   to meet his eyes. Green. Ava's green. Drowning.

<<avan "Leo.">>

His voice was low. Careful. The voice of someone handling something that might shatter.

<<leona "Yeah.">>

<<avan "We're going to be okay.">>

It wasn't a question. It wasn't a promise. It was the thing you say when you don't know if it's true but you need to hear it spoken aloud so the silence doesn't swallow you whole.

I reached up and took his hand. My fingers looked absurd against his   small, slim, swallowed entirely by his massive palm. He closed his grip gently   so gently, like he was already afraid of the strength the system had just warned him about   and the warmth of his skin against mine was the only thing in the room that felt like something I recognized.

Two pulses. Two rhythms. The neon veins in the walls read them both and wove them together in the same tangled pattern as before   Leona's fast and frightened, Avan's deep and unsteady   braided through the apartment's living walls like two heartbeats sharing one circulatory system.

The mirrors watched. The city glowed. The system counted down three minutes in silence.

Module 2 was coming.

[[Familiarization Module 2 Starting|day_5_15_str]]




:: day_5_15_str

<<system "Module 2: Center of Gravity and Basic Stance. Initiating.">>

The three minutes had passed like thirty seconds. I hadn't moved from where Avan and I stood between the platforms   hand in hand, bare feet on cold chrome, breathing the recycled neon-tinged air of an apartment that knew our heart rates better than we did. The holographic silhouettes from Module 1 had faded, but the mirrors hadn't. Three hundred sixty degrees of reflection. Every angle. No mercy.

New projections flickered to life   not beside us this time, but over us. Holographic overlays snapping directly onto our reflections in the mirror arc, mapping our postures in lines of pale blue light. Skeletal wireframes drawn over our skin. Joint markers glowing at shoulders, hips, knees, ankles and a single bright point   different color for each of us   marking our center of gravity.

Mine glowed amber. It sat low   alarmingly low   centered deep in my pelvis, right between my hip bones. I could see it pulsing gently in the mirror, nestled in the widest part of me like a stone resting at the bottom of a bowl.

Avan's glowed cyan. His sat high   chest-level, floating behind his sternum, perched above the massive architecture of his ribcage like a beacon mounted on a tower.

<<system "Center of gravity is the single most critical factor in movement, balance, and physical intuition. Both users' centers have shifted dramatically from their original bodies. Failure to internalize this shift is the primary cause of Stage 1 desync   motor disorientation, chronic imbalance, and cascading loss of proprioceptive confidence.">>

<<system "User Leona Hart. Your original center of gravity was located at approximately chest height   consistent with a lean male frame carrying the majority of its mass in the upper body. Your current center of gravity has dropped to pelvic level. You are now a bottom-heavy structure. Your hips are your anchor. Your chest is a counterweight, not a foundation.">>

I stared at the amber dot in the mirror. Tried to feel it. Tried to understand what bottom-heavy meant when every instinct in my brain was still calibrated for a body where my shoulders were the widest thing about me and my weight lived in my chest and arms.

<<system "Please release your partner's hand and stand naturally.">>

I didn't want to let go. Avan's grip tightened for a fraction of a second   the same reluctance, the same fear   and then his massive fingers loosened and my hand slid free. Cool air rushed across my palm where his warmth had been.

<<system "Feet shoulder-width apart. Arms at your sides. Face the primary mirror.">>

I turned to face the largest panel in the arc   dead center, floor-to-ceiling   and for the first time since the transformation, I looked at my full reflection without flinching away. The holographic wireframe overlay showed every misalignment. My shoulders were hiked up   tension, fear, instinct from a body that used to carry mass there. My knees were locked. My weight was pitched forward onto the balls of my feet, the way Leo used to stand   leaning slightly into the world, chest-first, ready to move.

Except chest-first didn't work anymore. I could feel it now that I was paying attention   the pull. Gravity dragging at the soft heavy weight on my chest, tugging me forward and down. My lower back was arching to compensate, which pushed my butt out behind me, which shifted my hips, which made my thighs tense in a way that felt wrong and unstable and 

I swayed. Not dramatically. Just a small, humiliating wobble   weight tipping forward, breasts shifting with the momentum, pulling me further off balance. I caught myself with a quick step and the impact sent a jiggle through my chest that the mirror reflected in excruciating detail.

<<leona "This is   I can't even stand still without ">>

<<system "You are attempting to balance using your original body's postural instincts. Those instincts are incorrect for this frame. Observe.">>

The holographic overlay on my reflection changed. A second wireframe appeared   translucent green   showing the correct posture superimposed over my current stance. The differences were stark. The green wireframe had its weight shifted back   settled into the heels rather than the balls of the feet. Hips centered directly beneath the amber gravity point. Shoulders dropped and slightly back. Core engaged   not the abs-forward brace Leo used, but a deeper stabilization, lower, wrapping around the pelvis like an internal girdle.

<<system "Your hips are now your primary load-bearing structure. Shift your weight backward. Allow your pelvis to center beneath you. Do not fight the forward pull of your chest   compensate from below, not above.">>

I tried. Rocked my weight back toward my heels. The shift was immediate and terrifying   like leaning backward over a ledge   because my brain was screaming that moving away from front-balance meant falling. But the amber dot in the mirror pulsed approvingly as it aligned with the green wireframe's target zone, and suddenly   oh. Something clicked. Not perfectly. Not comfortably. But the wobble steadied. My hips took the load. The heaviness on my chest stopped pulling me forward because the counterweight beneath   the wide, dense, curving architecture of my hips and thighs   had it anchored.

<<system "Better. Now drop your shoulders.">>

I tried. They barely moved. Every muscle from my neck to my shoulder blades was clenched in a rigid wall of tension.

<<system "Your trapezius and deltoid muscles are significantly smaller than your original body's. The tension you are holding serves no structural purpose   it is psychological. You are bracing as if your shoulders still carry your frame's primary mass. They do not. Release.">>

I exhaled   shaky, too loud in the quiet room   and forced my shoulders down. They dropped further than I expected. The wireframe overlay adjusted in real time, green lines aligning with my reflection as the posture settled. My arms hung differently   not straight at my sides but curving gently outward, displaced by the swell of my hips and the width of my chest. My elbows naturally angled away from my waist because there was body in the way that hadn't been there before.

<<system "Hold this position. Breathe normally.">>

I breathed. Each inhale lifted my chest   the weight rising, shifting, settling back down with a soft bounce that I could feel in every nerve the system had packed into the tissue. Each exhale let my ribs contract inward, waist narrowing, the holographic wireframe tracking the expansion and compression of a torso that moved like nothing I'd ever operated.

In the mirror, the correct-posture overlay and my reflection were nearly aligned. Nearly. The amber gravity point pulsed steady and low in my pelvis and for one strange, suspended moment I felt something that wasn't panic   something closer to recognition. This is how this body wants to stand. Not how I want to stand. How it wants to. Like the frame had opinions about its own architecture.

<<system "User Avan Morgan. Your turn.">>

Avan had been watching me   arms folded across his enormous chest, weight on one leg, the very picture of a man standing casually. Except the holographic wireframe over his reflection was screaming red at every joint. His cyan gravity point was floating above his sternum, high and forward, and his entire posture was a disaster of female instincts applied to a male body.

He was standing the way Ava stood. Weight centered in his hips   which were now narrow. Pelvis tilted forward   a habit from a body with a wider pelvic cradle that no longer existed. One hip cocked to the side, the way Ava shifted her weight when she was anxious. Except on a 6'3" frame carrying 188 pounds of stacked muscle, the cocked-hip stance looked like a building leaning on a cracked foundation.

<<system "Your original center of gravity was located at pelvic level   consistent with a petite female frame carrying proportional mass in the lower body. Your current center of gravity has risen to chest height. You are now a top-heavy structure. Your shoulders and torso are your primary mass. Your hips are a narrow pedestal supporting a disproportionately large upper frame.">>

<<system "Please uncross your arms and stand facing the primary mirror.">>

Avan unfolded his arms. The movement was too fast   the biceps flexing, shoulders rolling with a mechanical power that made the chrome floor vibrate beneath him. He turned to the mirror and froze.

The wireframe overlay showed everything wrong. His hips were tilted   the feminine anterior tilt that Ava's body used to balance a wider pelvis, now applied to a narrow male one. It pitched his massive torso forward, stacking all that shoulder and chest mass over a fulcrum point that couldn't support it. His knees were slightly together   female instinct, thighs closer   but his new thighs were too thick, too dense, and the inward angle made his stance pinch at the groin.

<<avan "I feel like I'm going to fall forward. Like my chest weighs a hundred pounds and there's nothing underneath to hold it.">>

<<system "That assessment is essentially correct. Your upper body mass has approximately tripled. Your lower body serves as a support column, not a counterbalance. You must widen your base.">>

The green correction wireframe appeared over his reflection. Feet wider   significantly wider, nearly to the edges of the platform circle. Pelvis neutral   not tilted forward, not tucked under, just stacked directly below the spine. Knees slightly bent, tracking over the toes. Thighs engaged   not for hip balance but for raw structural support, pillars holding a heavy ceiling.

<<system "Plant your feet at shoulder width or wider. Straighten your pelvis   eliminate the anterior tilt. Engage your quadriceps. Let your thighs bear the load.">>

Avan shifted. Widened his stance. The first correction   killing the hip tilt   made him visibly unstable for a moment, swaying backward as the mass that had been pitched forward suddenly had nowhere to go. His arms shot out   a balancing reflex   and the cyan gravity point in the mirror wobbled dangerously above his sternum.

<<avan "Whoa   whoa ">>

<<system "Bend your knees. Lower your frame. Do not stiffen.">>

He bent. Thighs flexing with obscene definition   every striated fiber standing out under bronze skin   and the sway arrested. The green wireframe overlay pulled closer to alignment. His shoulders settled back. The enormous shelf of his chest sat squarely over his core instead of cantilevering forward. His cyan dot steadied.

<<avan "Okay. Okay, that's   that's better. It feels like I'm squatting.">>

[[Continue Module 2|day_5_15_str_1]]

:: day_5_15_str_1

<<system "You are engaging musculature you have never possessed. The sensation will normalize. Your thighs are your foundation. Your core is your stabilizer. Your shoulders are cargo   do not attempt to balance with them.">>

<<leona "Shoulders are cargo… that's a nice way to put it.">>

The words slipped out before I could stop them   dry, automatic, the kind of deflection Leo used when things got too intense. Avan's eyes flicked to me in the mirror. The faintest twitch at the corner of his mouth. Not a smile. The ghost of one. The neon veins in the wall between us caught the shift in both our heart rates   a brief softening, twin pulses easing for half a second   before the system continued and the moment dissolved.

<<system "Now. Both users will perform a series of positional tests. Lean forward from the waist. Slowly.">>

I leaned. The weight on my chest swung forward immediately   heavy, pendulous, dragging my upper body down with a momentum that startled me. My hands flew out to catch myself against nothing. The wireframe overlay flashed red at my lower back   I was hyperextending, arching, every male instinct telling me to counterbalance with my spine instead of my hips.

<<system "User Leona. Do not arch. Hinge at the hips. Allow your pelvis to shift backward as your torso tilts forward   your gluteal mass will counterbalance your chest.">>

My gluteal mass. I was being told to use my ass as a counterweight. I shifted my hips back   felt the round, heavy curve of my backside push outward behind me as my torso dipped   and the balance point found itself. The amber dot held steady. The wireframe eased from red to green. In the mirror, the silhouette of the movement was so distinctly, unmistakably feminine   the arch of the back, the curve of the butt pushing out, the breasts hanging forward in the thin fabric   that I looked away. Face burning. The wall-veins flared pink.

Avan leaned forward and nearly toppled. All that mass   the chest, the shoulders, the arms   pitched over his narrow hips like a landslide over a cliff edge. His feet skidded on the chrome. He caught himself with one hand slamming down on the platform edge   the impact cracking through the room like a gunshot, chrome denting under his palm.

<<avan "God !">>

<<system "User Avan. Your forward lean must engage your posterior chain   hamstrings, glutes, spinal erectors. Brace from behind, not above. Your mass is too high for an unbraced forward tilt.">>

He straightened slowly. Breathing hard   each exhale a deep rumble that resonated in his barrel chest. The hand that had caught him left a visible impression in the chrome edge. He stared at the dent. Then at his palm. Then at the dent again.

<<avan "I just… dented the floor.">>

<<system "Your grip strength in this avatar exceeds 60 kilograms of force. Uncontrolled exertion will damage environment objects and potentially injure other users. This will be addressed further in Module 5.">>

He curled his fingers inward. Slow. Careful. Watching them close like he was watching someone else's hand from a great distance.

<<system "Lean backward. Slowly.">>

I rocked my weight onto my heels. This felt   better. Immediately, instinctively better. The heavy curve of my hips and backside settled beneath me like a foundation accepting a load. My chest rose slightly as my spine straightened, the weight lifting off my lower back. The amber gravity point sank deeper into my pelvis, right where the system said it belonged. In the mirror, the wireframe went solid green. Stable. Grounded. My body wasn't fighting gravity   it was sitting in it, like a stone in a river, heavy at the bottom and steady.

<<system "Note the difference, User Leona. Posterior lean engages your natural weight distribution. This body is designed to anchor from below. Forward motion requires conscious effort; backward stability is innate.">>

Avan leaned back and immediately stiffened. The mass of his upper body, tilted even slightly backward, pulled with terrifying force   like gravity had hooked fingers into his shoulder blades and yanked. His arms windmilled. His feet scrambled on chrome.

<<avan "No   no no no ">>

<<system "Engage your abdominal wall. Tighten your core. Your rectus abdominis must counterbalance your posterior mass during any backward lean.">>

He clenched   visible even through the thin fabric the system had given him   the ridged surface of his abdomen locking into a solid wall. The backward pull arrested. He hung there for a precarious second, every muscle in his torso firing, and then rocked forward to center. Gasping. Sweating at the temples, the chrome-bright light catching the sheen on his brow.

<<avan "Every direction wants to kill me. Forward I fall on my face. Backward I fall on my spine. How do men just   STAND?">>

<<system "Men do not 'just stand.' They have spent their entire developmental period calibrating for a high center of gravity. You have had approximately four minutes. The discrepancy is expected.">>

I almost laughed. Almost. The system's deadpan delivery hitting against Avan's genuine exasperation   it was the closest thing to humor this room had produced. But the sound that came out of me was a strange, breathy half-giggle that didn't sound anything like Leo's laugh, and I swallowed it fast. The wall-veins flickered   reading something between amusement and distress   and couldn't seem to decide on a color.

<<system "Final exercise. Both users: stand in corrected posture and hold for sixty seconds. The system will monitor stability.">>

I settled. Heels weighted. Hips centered. Shoulders dropped. Core engaged low   that strange, deep stabilization that wrapped around my pelvis like a band. The amber dot pulsed steady in the mirror. My breasts rose and fell with each breath   heavy, present, distracting   but the body held. Balanced. Alien and improbable and balanced.

Avan planted wide. Knees bent. Thighs braced. Pelvis neutral. The mountain of his upper body stacked straight over his core   not leaning, not fighting, just there. His cyan dot held high and steady behind his sternum. His hands hung at his sides, fingers deliberately open   consciously loose, consciously careful.

Sixty seconds in a room full of mirrors, holding poses that belonged to strangers. Our heart rates scrolled side by side on the bathroom's biometric display   Leona's at 94, fast but falling. Avan's at 81, deep and stabilizing. The neon veins in the walls wove them together in slow, tangled patterns   violet and cyan threading through charcoal panels like circulatory art.

<<system "Stability threshold met. Module 2 complete.">>

The holographic wireframes dissolved. The green correction overlays faded. The mirrors remained   of course they did   but without the clinical annotations, they were just reflections again. Just a small woman with too many curves and a large man with too much muscle, standing six feet apart in a floating apartment, learning how to not fall over in bodies they didn't choose.

<<system "Module 3 will begin in ninety seconds. Subject: Walking and Movement. Please use this interval to practice corrected stance independently.">>

Avan took a single step toward me and the chrome boomed under his foot. He flinched. Looked down. Took another step   lighter this time, but still heavy, still trembling with restrained force   and stopped at arm's reach. His hand lifted. Hesitated. Then settled on top of my head   gently, so gently, his enormous palm barely making contact with my hair. The honey-brown strands shifted under his touch and the sensation rippled across my scalp with a vividness that made my breath catch.

<<avan "You're doing good, Leo.">>

His voice. Low. Ava's gentleness poured through a bass register that made my ribs hum. I tilted my head up   way, way up   and met his eyes. Green. Scared. Warm.

<<leona "You too, Ava.">>

Ninety seconds. The city pulsed beyond the glass. The mirrors watched. Somewhere inside the system's cold architecture, a timer counted down toward Module 3, and two people who'd never walked in these bodies practiced standing still   because standing was hard enough.

[[Familiarization Module 3 Starting|day_5_16_str]]


