<<system "Module 3: Walking and Movement. Initiating.">>

A line of light split the apartment floor.

It appeared without warning   a neon pathway burning to life in the chrome, running the full length of the room from the platform circles to the far wall of windows. Violet at the edges, white at the center, approximately one meter wide. Straight. Clinical. A runway.

On either side, the holographic mirrors adjusted   angling inward to track the pathway, ensuring that every step taken along its length would be reflected from at least four directions simultaneously. The apartment's climate system held still. No warm currents. No hair-lifting breeze. Just the flat, antiseptic air of a testing environment.

<<system "Walking is the most fundamental expression of your avatar's biomechanics. An incorrect gait will compound postural errors, accelerate muscle fatigue, and increase the probability of desync through chronic proprioceptive mismatch. Both users will walk the illuminated pathway. Holographic gait correction will overlay your reflections in real time.">>

<<system "User Leona Hart. You will proceed first. Approach the start of the pathway.">>

I looked at the glowing line stretching across the chrome floor. Fifteen meters, maybe. The length of a long hallway. The easiest thing in the world   or it had been, in a body that didn't shift and sway and pull at every joint with unfamiliar weight.

I stepped off the platform circle. Bare feet on cold chrome. My first step was instinctive   long, straight, the way Leo walked. Heel-strike, weight rolling forward to the toe, arms swinging loosely at my sides, stride covering roughly seventy centimeters without thinking.

The holographic wireframe on the nearest mirror went red before my foot landed.

Pain shot through my inner thigh   not sharp, but deep, a pulling ache that radiated from the hip socket down through muscle I didn't know I had. My pelvis twisted on the stride   the wider structure forced to rotate farther than Leo's narrow hips ever needed to   and the rotation sent a ripple up through my waist, my ribcage, my chest. My breasts swung with the momentum. Left, then right. Heavy. Visible. The weight shifted my balance mid-stride and I stumbled sideways, catching myself with a lurching half-step that made everything bounce twice.

<<leona "Nnh !">>

<<system "Incorrect. You are attempting a male gait pattern   long stride, lateral arm swing, shoulder-led momentum. This pattern is mechanically incompatible with your current skeletal structure.">>

The wireframe replay appeared in the mirror   my failed stride dissected in slow-motion holographic overlay. Every error highlighted. The too-long step forcing my hip joint past its comfortable rotation. The pelvic twist cascading upward. The unsupported breast tissue oscillating with a momentum diagram drawn in dotted red lines showing exactly how the weight had pulled me off axis.

I wanted to die. Not literally. Just   the specific, burning, full-body mortification of watching a detailed physics analysis of your own breasts bouncing displayed in high-definition holographic replay across four mirrors.

<<system "Your pelvis is wider and shallower than your original body's. The femoral angle   the angle at which your thigh bones connect to your hips   has changed. Your legs now converge slightly toward a center line rather than tracking in parallel. This is structural, not behavioral. A wide male stride forces your hip joints into excessive rotation, creating discomfort and instability.">>

The green correction wireframe appeared   the right way to walk in this body. The differences were immediately, viscerally obvious.

Shorter stride. Significantly shorter   maybe fifty centimeters instead of seventy. Feet tracking closer to the center line, nearly heel-to-toe on a single invisible rail rather than on two parallel tracks and the hips   the wireframe's hips moved. Not dramatically, not performatively, but with a lateral shift on each step   a gentle sway that the system annotated with a small, damning label: biomechanically unavoidable at this hip-to-waist ratio.

<<system "The lateral pelvic shift you see in the correction model is not optional. It is a mechanical consequence of your hip width relative to your waist. When your foot strikes the ground, your pelvis must shift to center your weight over the supporting leg. The narrower the waist relative to the hips, the more visible this shift becomes. At your proportions   60 centimeter waist, 98 centimeter hips   the shift will be pronounced.">>

<<leona "You're telling me I have to   that the   the SWAY is just ">>

<<system "Physics. Yes.">>

I closed my eyes. Breathed. Opened them. Walked.

Shorter stride this time. Feet closer together and I felt it immediately   the hip shift. Not something I was doing. Something the body did. Each step forced a transfer of weight from one side to the other, and the wide cradle of my pelvis tilted with the transfer, and the tilt traveled up through my waist and made my hips move in a rolling, rhythmic oscillation that I could see in every mirror.

Left step   hips shift right. Right step   hips shift left. The motion was smooth, fluid, continuous and everything attached to that motion moved with it. My butt swayed   round, heavy, each cheek alternating in a soft rise-and-fall that the thin synth-fabric did absolutely nothing to conceal. My waist flexed   the narrow bridge between my ribcage and hips bending and straightening with each lateral tilt. Even my hair moved   the long honey-brown waves swinging gently across my lower back in counterrhythm to my stride.

I made it five steps before the other problem became undeniable.

My breasts.

Every step sent a shockwave upward through my frame   heel-strike, impact, vibration traveling up through my legs, my hips, my torso   and the moment that vibration reached my chest, the weight there caught it and amplified it. Bounce. Every single step. Not subtle. Not ignorable. Each stride produced a visible, physical, oscillating bounce that I could feel in excruciating detail   the tissue rising on impact, hanging for a fraction of a second at the apex, then dropping back down with a soft, heavy settling that tugged at the skin above my sternum and sent sensation radiating through nerve endings packed tighter than any other region of my body.

By the seventh step my arms had crossed instinctively over my chest   pressing inward, trying to contain the movement   and the moment I did that, my stride shortened further, my shoulders hunched, and the wireframe in the mirror lit up with new corrections.

<<system "User Leona. Arm positioning.">>

The green wireframe adjusted. My original arm swing   wide, loose, swinging from the shoulders   was replaced with something more contained. Arms closer to the body. Elbows slightly bent. Forearms angled gently outward rather than hanging straight, because straight-hanging arms collided with the lateral swell of my hips and the forward projection of my chest. The wireframe showed a natural arm path that curved   arcing around the bust, clearing the hips, a gentler, more compact motion than Leo's rangy swing.

<<system "Your chest projection and hip width preclude a straight-arm swing. Your arms must track a path that accommodates your torso's profile. The adjustment feels restrictive at first. It will normalize. Additionally   crossing your arms over your chest while walking disrupts your gait timing and compresses breast tissue uncomfortably. Supportive garments will be addressed in Module 9. For now, allow natural movement.">>

Allow natural movement. Allow the bouncing. Just   let it happen. Let every step produce that visible, heavy, swaying oscillation of a chest that weighed more than a kilogram and was packed with enough nerve endings to feel every microsecond of its own momentum.

I uncrossed my arms. Placed them at my sides in the corrected position   slightly bent, gently curved, clearing the body's profile and I walked. The full length of the pathway. Fifteen meters of neon-lit chrome, bare feet padding in short, hip-swaying steps, breasts bouncing with every stride, hair swishing across my back, the mirrors showing me a woman who moved like water   fluid, rolling, rhythmic   and the person inside that woman gripping the shattered remnants of his composure with both hands.

The wireframe went green. Full alignment. Correct gait acquired.

I reached the end of the pathway and stopped. Breathing harder than fifteen meters of flat walking should have warranted. Face burning. The pulse-veins in the wall nearest me glowed a deep, mortified rose.

<<system "Gait pattern accepted. User Leona Hart demonstrates functional female ambulation. Proceed to the end of the pathway and wait.">>

I was already at the end. I turned   the motion producing another cascade of shifting, bouncing, swaying that I was starting to understand I would simply have to live with   and watched Avan.

<<system "User Avan Morgan. Approach the start of the pathway.">>

He stepped forward. Ava's first step   small, precise, hips leading, the delicate foot-placement of a woman who'd spent nineteen years navigating the world in a body a third this size.

The chrome boomed.

His foot hit the floor with a force that sent vibrations radiating outward through the pulse-veins. The small, dainty step covered barely forty centimeters   absurd for legs that were nearly a meter long. His hips swayed dramatically   the female pattern, the lateral shift Ava had used her entire life   but on a frame with narrow hips and enormous shoulders, the sway looked wrong. Top-heavy. The mass above his waist lurched with each tiny step, shoulders rocking side to side like a ship in rough water, arms swinging in tight arcs that bumped against his own ribcage.

He looked like a building trying to tiptoe.

<<system "Incorrect. You are applying a female gait pattern to a male frame. Your stride is too short for your leg length. Your hip sway exceeds what your pelvic structure produces   you are adding movement that is no longer biomechanically generated. Your arm swing is too compact for your shoulder width.">>

The wireframe replay was brutal. Avan's tiny steps highlighted against the green correction model   which showed a stride nearly double the length, feet tracking on two parallel lines rather than one center rail. The green wireframe's hips barely moved laterally   a narrow pelvis didn't need the weight-transfer shift. Instead, the shoulders led. The momentum came from above   a forward lean from the chest, arms swinging in wide arcs that used the shoulder mass as pendulums, each swing building momentum into the next stride.

<<system "Male gait is shoulder-dominant. Your stride length should match your inseam   approximately 85 centimeters. Plant heel-first with full weight. Let your shoulders lead your momentum. Arms swing wide to counterbalance your upper mass. Your hips are passive   they follow, they do not drive.">>

Avan stared at the correction model. Swallowed. Set his jaw.

He took a step. A real step. Long, deliberate, heel cracking against chrome with a resonance that rumbled through the floor. The stride was wide   his thick thighs clearing each other with the parallel tracking the system demanded   and the impact was enormous. The chrome vibrated. The pulse-veins flickered with the shockwave. His body pitched forward with the momentum   shoulders leading, chest driving   and his arms swung out in wide, powerful arcs that cut the air with visible force.

One stride covered the distance my body needed three steps to cross.

<<avan "Oh ">>

Another step. Longer. More confident. The wireframe tightened toward green alignment. His shoulders rolled with a natural cadence   the massive deltoids and trapezius muscles doing exactly what the system said they would, converting arm swing into forward drive. Each footfall was a controlled detonation   weight transferred cleanly through his leg, absorbed by the quad and calf, released into the next push. He was fast. Two steps and he'd already crossed a quarter of the pathway.

Three steps. Four. The wireframe went fully green. He was walking correctly   not gracefully, not naturally, but correctly   and the sheer mechanical efficiency of it was staggering. Ava Morgan, who used to take two steps for every one of Leo's, was now eating distance like the floor owed her money.

Then he reached the wardrobe.

The open wardrobe   standing perpendicular to the pathway, its illuminated racks jutting out into the walking space   occupied a gap that Avan's brain registered as "plenty of room." Because it was plenty of room. For Ava. For a body 38 centimeters across at the shoulders. For a frame that could slip through spaces sideways without thinking.

