Avan's shoulders were 52 centimeters wide. Plus arms. Plus the broad-shouldered cyber-leather jacket hanging from the nearest rack.

He clipped the wardrobe at full stride. His right shoulder smashed into the rack with a metallic crash that sent hangers spinning, a neon-mesh bodysuit flying, and the illuminated rack swaying on its mount. Avan staggered sideways   overcorrected   his left shoulder caught the edge of the hovering bed frame and he spun, off-balance, his massive body careening into the apartment's curved wall with a thud that made the neon veins behind the impact point flare white.

<<avan "Ow   what   I had ROOM ">>

<<system "You did not. Your spatial awareness is calibrated for a body 38 centimeters wide at the shoulder. Your current body is 52 centimeters. You must add approximately 14 centimeters of clearance to every spatial judgment you make. Doorways, corridors, furniture gaps, and proximity to other individuals all require recalculation.">>

He peeled himself off the wall. Rubbed his shoulder   which had left a visible scuff on the matte charcoal panel. The wardrobe rack swung gently behind him, the neon-mesh bodysuit puddled on the floor like a fallen flag.

I pressed my lips together. Hard. The corners twitched. In my chest something bubbled   dangerous, involuntary, tickling my ribs   and I clamped down on it with every ounce of willpower because laughing at Avan right now would be cruel and also I was standing at the end of a catwalk with bouncing breasts and swaying hips and I had absolutely no moral high ground.

A tiny sound escaped anyway. A breath. A squeak. Something that my new vocal cords shaped into a noise so soft and feminine it barely existed.

